---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';

const faqItems = [
  {"question": "Is Muse Code the same as the Muse personal agent?", "answer": "No. Muse Code is a separate developer product. Its Everyday Usage, High Usage, Power Usage, and Maximum Usage plans are not the personal agent\u2019s Free, Power, and Maximum plans."},

  {
    question: 'What is Meta Muse in one sentence?',
    answer:
      'Meta Muse, also commonly written MetaMuse, is a personal AI agent from Meta, released on September 8, 2026, that carries out online tasks for a user instead of only returning a written answer.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is Meta Muse different from a chatbot?',
    answer:
      'A chatbot primarily returns text that the user must act on. Meta Muse is designed to perform tasks such as browsing pages, filling forms, placing orders, or sending email, and it runs in a separate cloud virtual machine.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Meta Muse run on my phone or in the cloud?',
    answer:
      "Muse has a cloud browser in Muse Secure VM. Muse for Mac can also use local files and apps such as Messages, Notes, and Calendar after system authorization. Web, mobile, Mac, and WhatsApp provide entry points where available.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Meta Muse part of Meta AI?',
    answer:
      'No. Muse is a separate personal agent available through web, mobile, Mac, and WhatsApp entry points. It is not the same product as Meta AI.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When did Meta Muse launch?',
    answer:
      'Meta released Meta Muse on September 8, 2026. Muse and its subscriptions remain in limited testing and are not available everywhere.',
  },
];

const relatedLinks = [
  {
    label: 'Meta Muse explained',
    href: '/',
    description: 'A practical overview of capabilities, pricing, setup, and privacy questions.',
  },
  {
    label: 'how to use Meta Muse',
    href: '/how-to-use-meta-muse/',
    description: 'Setup steps for the app, plan selection, connected services, and first tasks.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Meta Muse pricing',
    href: '/meta-muse-pricing/',
    description: 'Compare Free, Power, and Maximum with official weekly token allowances.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Meta Muse review',
    href: '/meta-muse-review/',
    description: 'An early assessment of where the agent works well and where it is limited.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="What Is Meta Muse? Meta's Personal AI Agent Explained"
  description="Meta Muse is a personal AI agent released on September 8, 2026. It uses a cloud computer and, with permission, local Mac apps. Compare Muse, Meta AI, Muse Image/Video, and Muse Code."
  canonicalPath="/what-is-meta-muse/"
  pageType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'What Is Meta Muse?' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="content">
    <p class="eyebrow">Product guide</p>
    <h1>What Is Meta Muse?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Meta Muse is a personal AI agent built for ordinary online tasks. Its defining idea is
      action: the product is meant to complete work, not only describe how to do it.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="definition-heading">
      <h2 id="definition-heading">Meta Muse in One Sentence</h2>
      <p>
        Meta Muse, also commonly written MetaMuse, is a personal AI agent from Meta, released on September 8, 2026, that carries
        out online tasks for a user instead of only answering questions. It can browse webpages,
        fill forms, place orders, send email, and use Connectors such as Gmail and Google Calendar.
        Muse and its subscriptions are in limited testing and are not globally available.
        Access starts at <a href="https://muse.ai/">muse.ai</a> or the standalone Muse app.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="difference-heading">
      <h2 id="difference-heading">How It Differs From a Chatbot</h2>
      <p>
        The simplest distinction is the final output. A chatbot usually gives the user words to
        work with, while Meta Muse is designed to produce an action or a finished result. The
        table below separates that difference from the environment where each kind of tool works.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Chatbot compared with Meta Muse</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Question</th>
              <th scope="col">Chatbot</th>
              <th scope="col">Meta Muse</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What it returns</th>
              <td>A written answer or explanation.</td>
              <td>The result of a task it performed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Where it runs</th>
              <td>Inside a conversation interface.</td>
              <td>Cloud VM, plus authorized local access through the Mac app.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What you do after</th>
              <td>Read the response and carry out the next step yourself.</td>
              <td>Review the action, order, email, or other result.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        In this context, an agent is software that can take a goal and perform steps toward it.
        That does not make it independent in the human sense: it still needs instructions,
        access, and a website or service that permits the action. The practical shift is that
        more of the work moves from the user's hands to the agent's browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="runs-heading">
      <h2 id="runs-heading">Where Meta Muse Runs</h2>
      <p>
        Muse’s cloud browser runs in a separate virtual machine called Muse Secure VM. Meta says the
        environment provides enough storage, CPU, and memory for real work, along with its own
        browser. It is isolated from the user's local computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cloud browser is one part of Muse. <a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/download/">Muse for Mac</a>
        can also use local files and apps after macOS authorization. You can chat with Muse on the
        web, mobile, Mac, or in WhatsApp where available. Cloud isolation does not describe the
        separate permissions you may grant on your Mac.
      </p>
      <p>
        Review Connector access, including automatic Facebook, Instagram, and Threads connections
        for accounts in the same Accounts Center. Meta says you can change
        access, disconnect services, and opt out of interactions being used for model training.
        Sensitive Connector writes need approval each time. Media allegations about message access
        are discussed separately from these official controls in the
        <a href="/meta-muse-review/#privacy-heading">Meta Muse privacy discussion</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="meta-ai-heading">
      <h2 id="meta-ai-heading">How Meta Muse Relates to Meta AI</h2>
      <p>
        Meta Muse and Meta AI have separate entry points. Meta Muse is available as an independent
        iOS app, Android app, Mac app, and web product, with WhatsApp chat also available.
        It should be evaluated separately from Meta AI and other products with Muse in their names.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distinction is practical for anyone comparing tools: opening Meta AI does not mean
        you are using Meta Muse, and the two products should be evaluated separately. This site
        covers the Muse agent, its pricing, setup process, cloud environment, and current trust
        concerns.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="product-names-heading">
      <h2 id="product-names-heading">Muse, Muse Image/Video, and Muse Code</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table>
        <caption>Which Muse product does a search result describe?</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">What it refers to</th><th scope="col">Plan distinction</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Muse personal agent</th><td>The task agent covered by this site.</td><td><a href="/meta-muse-pricing/">Free, Power, and Maximum</a>; weekly Muse token allowances.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Muse Image / Muse Video</th><td>Image and video generation offerings.</td><td>Names alone do not establish personal-agent subscription benefits.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Muse Code</th><td>A separate developer coding product and subscription.</td><td>Everyday Usage, High Usage, Power Usage, and Maximum Usage.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p><a href="https://dev.meta.ai/docs/muse-code/subscriptions">Muse Code subscription documentation</a>
        uses its own usage rules. Power Usage and Maximum Usage are not the personal agent’s
        Power and Maximum weekly-token plans. Do not transfer pricing or benefits between them.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="timeline-heading">
      <h2 id="timeline-heading">Timeline</h2>
      <p>
        The launch date below is separate from our documentation review date; the latter does not
        establish when an individual feature first became available.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Verified Meta Muse timeline</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Date</th>
              <th scope="col">Event</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time></th>
              <td>Meta releases Meta Muse, with the initial release in the United States.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><time datetime="2026-10-29">October 29, 2026</time></th>
              <td>Documentation reviewed for Mac access, Connectors, WhatsApp, security status, and Muse Code naming.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="fit-heading">
      <h2 id="fit-heading">Who Should Try It, Who Should Wait</h2>
      <p>
        Meta Muse is most relevant to people who want an online task carried out rather than
        another conversational answer. The decision also depends on location and how much
        personal data you are prepared to connect.
      </p>

      <h3>Who Should Try It</h3>
      <p>
        It may be a good fit if Muse is available to your account and you regularly deal with web-based
        tasks. You may also be a reasonable early user if you are willing to connect Gmail or
        Google Calendar for work that requires email and schedule access. Starting with the free
        tier can show whether the workflow matches your needs before you pay.
      </p>

      <h3>Who Should Wait</h3>
      <p>
        Waiting is sensible if Muse is unavailable in your location, you need only text-based answers,
        or you are uncomfortable connecting email and calendar data. Review both the reported
        privacy allegations and Meta’s published explanation of user controls.
        Anyone with a sensitive account should review the access requirements before handing over
        a task.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <h2 id="sources-heading">Official Sources</h2>
      <p>Capabilities, access, security status, and product names checked <time datetime="2026-10-29">2026-10-29</time>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/subscriptions/1021145227643680/">Meta Help: subscriptions and availability</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/">Meta: launch, cloud VM, privacy, and training controls</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ai.meta.com/muse/download/">Muse for Mac and mobile downloads</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1126304576638594/">Mac files and app access</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1687253048996149/">Connectors and automatic Meta connections</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.meta.com/help/superintelligence/1047255454427887/">Privacy, safety, and security</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://platform.muse.ai/docs">Meta: Connector permissions and sensitive-action approval</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <FAQ items={faqItems} title="Frequently Asked Questions" />

    <RelatedLinks links={relatedLinks} />
  </article>
</Base>
